  "prompt_text": "The figure compares a thunderstorm in the current climate with one in a future climate, focusing on hail growth, rain, and winds inside the storm. In both panels, a strong updraft rises through the cloud while winds increase with height, creating vertical wind shear between near-surface wind and stronger 6-km wind aloft. Red horizontal lines mark the melting level height (MLH) and the very cold level near −40 °C.\n\nThe main visual message is that in the future climate the storm appears deeper and the melting level is higher. More supercooled liquid water and larger hailstones are shown being carried upward through the cloud, suggesting enhanced hail growth in the updraft region. At the same time, more rain is depicted lower in the storm. The hail trajectory is also drawn differently, implying that the balance between updraft strength, melting, and wind transport changes.\n\nThe numbered markers in the future panel highlight the key shifts: conditions near the surface, around the higher melting level, and aloft near stronger upper-level flow appear altered, with a question mark indicating some uncertainty in the high-level response. Overall, the figure conveys how warming may change storm structure and potentially favor larger hail before melting.",
